  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Transportation obligated $318M in FY2027 to date. The Department of Transportation recorded 1,516 awards with an average award size of $210K, and obligations fell 97.8% year over year. Historical annual obligations show a large prior-year scale: FY22 was $8B, FY23 was $8.7B, FY24 was $8.6B, FY25 was $9B, and FY26 was $14.1B. The current-year total is partial because figures are fiscal year to date. Engineering Services dominated FY2027 to date obligations at $94.2M, followed by Computer Systems Design Services at $41M and Ship Building and Repairing at $38.3M. Vendor concentration in FY2027 to date includes CROWLEY GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. at $40.7M and GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $30.8M. The top ten NAICS categories and top ten vendors together account for the majority of the $318M in obligations reported so far for FY2027 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did Transportation's FY2027 spending compare to prior years?",
        "body": "The Department of Transportation obligated $318M in FY2027 to date. FY26 obligations were $14.1B, much larger than the current-year to date total. FY25 obligations were $9B, FY24 obligations were $8.6B, FY23 obligations were $8.7B, and FY22 obligations were $8B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are the largest contract categories for FY2027 to date?",
        "body": "Engineering Services is the largest category at $94.2M. Computer Systems Design Services is second at $41M, and Ship Building and Repairing is third at $38.3M. Telecommunications Resellers, Other Computer Related Services, and Software Publishers follow with $21.3M, $20.5M, and $9.9M respectively."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Transportation spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Transportation obligated $318M in FY2027 to date. The Department of Transportation recorded 1,516 awards with an average award size of $210K, and obligations fell 97.8% year over year. Historical annual obligations were $8B in FY22, $8.7B in FY23, $8.6B in FY24, $9B in FY25, and $14.1B in FY26."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Transportation buy?",
      "a": "The Department of Transportation buys professional and technical services and transportation-related goods and services. Engineering Services led FY2027 to date obligations at $94.2M, followed by Computer Systems Design Services at $41M and Ship Building and Repairing at $38.3M. Additional categories in the top ten include Telecommunications Resellers at $21.3M and Other Computer Related Services at $20.5M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Transportation's largest contractors?",
      "a": "CROWLEY GOVERNMENT SERVICES, INC. is the largest contractor in FY2027 to date at $40.7M. GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. is next at $30.8M, followed by HARRIS CORPORATION at $21.3M and RAYTHEON COMPANY at $15.9M. Other top vendors include SCIENCE APPLICATIONS INTERNATIONAL CORPORATION at $14.5M and ARINC INCORPORATED at $10.6M."
    }
